$WW,1$$FG,5$$TX+CX,"YTracker"$$FG$
$TX+CX,"Sample tracker for YDE"$

YTracker writes music the way trackers have since the Amiga: a grid of notes scrolling downward, one column per voice. It plays $FG,2$.MOD$FG$, $FG,2$.XM$FG$, $FG,2$.S3M$FG$, $FG,2$.IT$FG$ and $FG,2$.FTM$FG$ modules, and saves its own $FG,2$YTK3$FG$ songs.

Press $FG,2$F1$FG$ in the app for the key list. This document is the long version.

$FG,8$Format is detected from the file's contents, never its extension. Renaming a module does not change what it is.$FG$

$HL,1$$TR,"1. How a tracker works"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
If you have used a piano roll, a tracker is the same idea turned ninety degrees and made discrete. Time runs $FG,2$downward$FG$, not rightward, and everything lands on an exact row rather than wherever you dragged it.

Four things stack up:

	$FG,2$Sample$FG$	 A recorded sound. One drum hit, one bass note.
	$FG,2$Instrument$FG$ A sample plus how it should behave: envelopes,
			 which sample to use for which key, how it fades.
	$FG,2$Pattern$FG$	 A grid of rows and channels. Usually 64 rows.
	$FG,2$Order$FG$	 The playlist. Which pattern plays, and in what
			 sequence. Patterns can repeat.

A pattern does not have a fixed duration. It has $FG,2$rows$FG$, and how fast rows go by is set by two numbers:

	$FG,2$Speed$FG$	Ticks per row. Default 6.
	$FG,2$Tempo$FG$	Rows per minute, in BPM. Default 125.

A $FG,2$tick$FG$ is the smallest unit of time. Notes start on tick 0 of a row; effects like slides and vibrato run on the ticks in between. That is why a slide sounds smooth even though notes are on a grid: there are six chances per row to move the pitch.

Lower speed means fewer ticks per row, so rows pass faster. Raising tempo does the same thing more directly. Most songs set both once at the start and never touch them again.
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"2. The screen"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
	$FG,2$Top line$FG$	 Song title, edit or view mode, transport state.
	$FG,2$Second line$FG$ Status messages. What just loaded, what failed.
	$FG,2$Third line$FG$	 Tempo, pattern, row, order, octave, instrument, gain.
	$FG,2$Scope$FG$		 $FG,2$Click it$FG$ to cycle three modes; the
			 choice is saved.

			 $FG,2$Split$FG$	left lane above right, each in its
				own accent colour.
			 $FG,2$Mono$FG$	the sum, across the full box.
			 $FG,2$Over$FG$	both superimposed on one axis, a
				trace each. Where the channels agree the traces
				sit on each other and one colour shows, which
				is the picture: agreeing is what the signal is
				doing. Where they part the lines separate and
				both colours appear.

			 Labels sit down the left edge: $FG,2$L$FG$ and
			 $FG,2$R$FG$ against the lane each channel owns, as a
			 colour key when they share one, and $FG,2$MONO$FG$
			 when there is only the sum.

			 Every mode draws a connected trace: each column
			 contributes its mean, joined to the previous one,
			 over a bar spanning that column's minimum to maximum.
			 The line is what makes it a wave rather than a row of
			 loose dots. The trace is triggered on a rising zero
			 crossing, so it stands still instead of reshuffling
			 every frame. Both accent colours come from Settings,
			 Appearance.
	$FG,2$Position bar$FG$ The whole song as one bar. See below.
	$FG,2$Channel heads$FG$ CH01, CH02... with a VU meter under each.
		A channel shows its name when the format supplies one.
		None of MOD, XM, S3M or IT names channels; FTM does
		not either, but its channels have fixed roles, so an
		FTM shows $FG,2$CH03 Triangle$FG$, $FG,2$CH06 VRC6 Saw$FG$ and so on.
	$FG,2$The grid$FG$	 Row number on the left, then one cell per channel.

The $FG,2$position bar$FG$ maps the order list across the window. Each order gets an equal slice, and the slice fills as the pattern plays, so it creeps rather than jumping. $FG,2$Click anywhere on it to seek there.$FG$ Clicking while stopped just moves the cursor; clicking while playing restarts playback from that order.

Click a $FG,2$channel header$FG$ to mute or unmute that channel. Click a $FG,2$cell$FG$ to put the cursor on it.
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"3. Reading a cell"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
Every cell is thirteen characters in four fields:

	$FG,2$C-4 01 40 A08$FG$
	$FG,8$ ^   ^  ^  ^$FG$
	$FG,8$ |   |  |  effect and its parameter$FG$
	$FG,8$ |   |  volume column$FG$
	$FG,8$ |   instrument number$FG$
	$FG,8$ note and octave$FG$

A dot means empty. An empty field is not "zero", it is "do not change" - an empty volume column leaves the volume alone rather than silencing the note.

$FG,5$Note$FG$
	$FG,2$C-4$FG$	Note name, sharp marker, octave. C-0 to B-9.
	$FG,2$^^-$FG$	Note off. Releases the note; envelopes fade it out.
	$FG,2$...$FG$	Nothing on this row.

	Middle C is $FG,2$C-4$FG$. That is the note XM, S3M and IT all tune
	to 8363 Hz, so a MOD loaded here shows the same octaves it
	shows in OpenMPT.

$FG,5$Instrument$FG$
	Two hex digits, $FG,2$01$FG$ to $FG,2$FF$FG$. Selects which instrument plays.

	Writing an instrument with no note does not start a sound. It
	loads that instrument's default volume onto the channel. Old
	Amiga modules lean on this constantly.

$FG,5$Volume column$FG$
	Two hex digits, $FG,2$00$FG$ to $FG,2$40$FG$, so 64 is full volume.

	XM and IT also put commands here, shown as a letter plus one
	digit: $FG,2$v$FG$ slide down, $FG,2$d$FG$ slide up, $FG,2$u$FG$ fine down, $FG,2$f$FG$ fine up,
	$FG,2$S$FG$ vibrato speed, $FG,2$P$FG$ vibrato depth, $FG,2$p$FG$ set pan, $FG,2$L$FG$ pan left,
	$FG,2$R$FG$ pan right, $FG,2$G$FG$ tone portamento.

$FG,5$Effect$FG$
	One digit of command, two of parameter. See the next section.
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"4. Effect reference"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
The parameter is usually two hex digits, and they are often read as two separate nibbles, written $FG,2$xy$FG$ below.

$FG,5$Pitch$FG$
	$FG,2$0xy$FG$	Arpeggio. Cycles note, note+x, note+y every tick.
		The chiptune chord.
	$FG,2$1xx$FG$	Portamento up. Slides pitch up xx per tick.
	$FG,2$2xx$FG$	Portamento down.
	$FG,2$3xx$FG$	Tone portamento. Slides toward the note in this cell
		at speed xx instead of retriggering it. Put a note in
		the cell to aim it, then $FG,2$300$FG$ to keep sliding.
	$FG,2$4xy$FG$	Vibrato. x is speed, y is depth.
	$FG,2$5xy$FG$	Tone portamento plus volume slide xy.
	$FG,2$6xy$FG$	Vibrato plus volume slide xy.

$FG,5$Volume and panning$FG$
	$FG,2$7xy$FG$	Tremolo. Volume wobble. x speed, y depth.
	$FG,2$8xx$FG$	Set panning, 00 left to FF right.
	$FG,2$Axy$FG$	Volume slide. x up per tick, or y down per tick.
	$FG,2$Cxx$FG$	Set volume, 00 to 40.

$FG,5$Sample and timing$FG$
	$FG,2$9xx$FG$	Sample offset. Start xx*256 frames in. Good for
		skipping the attack of a long sample.
	$FG,2$Bxx$FG$	Position jump. Jump to order xx.
	$FG,2$Dxx$FG$	Pattern break. Stop here, continue at row xx of the
		next order.
	$FG,2$Fxx$FG$	Below 20 hex sets speed, at or above sets tempo.

$FG,5$Extended, the E commands$FG$
	$FG,2$E1x$FG$ $FG,2$E2x$FG$	Fine portamento up and down. Once, not per tick.
	$FG,2$E3x$FG$		Glissando. Tone portamento snaps to semitones.
	$FG,2$E4x$FG$ $FG,2$E7x$FG$	Vibrato and tremolo waveform.
	$FG,2$E5x$FG$		Set finetune.
	$FG,2$E6x$FG$		Pattern loop. E60 marks the start, E6x repeats x times.
	$FG,2$E9x$FG$		Retrigger the note every x ticks.
	$FG,2$EAx$FG$ $FG,2$EBx$FG$	Fine volume up and down.
	$FG,2$ECx$FG$		Note cut at tick x.
	$FG,2$EDx$FG$		Note delay. Start the note on tick x instead of 0.
	$FG,2$EEx$FG$		Pattern delay. Repeat this row x more times.

$FG,5$From XM, S3M and IT$FG$
	These have no single-digit MOD equivalent. They arrive when you
	load a module that uses them.

	Global volume and global volume slide, channel volume and channel
	volume slide, panning slide, panbrello, tremor, multi retrigger
	with a volume table, fine vibrato, extra fine portamento, key off,
	set envelope position, and a separate set-tempo command.
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"5. Keys"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
$FG,5$Entering notes$FG$
	The two keyboard rows are a piano. The lower row starts at the
	current octave, the upper row an octave above.

	$FG,2$Z S X D C V G B H N J M$FG$	 lower octave
	$FG,2$Q 2 W 3 E R 5 T 6 Y 7 U$FG$	 upper octave

	$FG,2$1$FG$	Note off.
	$FG,2$0$FG$	Clear the cell.
	$FG,2$Backspace$FG$ or $FG,2$Del$FG$	Clear the cell.

	In the other columns the same keys type hex digits, $FG,2$0-9$FG$ and
	$FG,2$A-F$FG$. The cursor advances when a field is full.

$FG,5$Moving$FG$
	$FG,2$Arrows$FG$		 Move by sub-column. Left and right step
				 through note, instrument, volume, effect.
	$FG,2$Tab$FG$ $FG,2$Shift-Tab$FG$	 Next and previous channel.
	$FG,2$Ctrl-Left/Right$FG$ Channel.
	$FG,2$Ctrl-Up/Down$FG$	 Pattern.
	$FG,2$Shift-Up/Down$FG$	 Instrument.
	$FG,2$Shift-Left/Right$FG$ Octave.
	$FG,2$PgUp$FG$ $FG,2$PgDn$FG$	 Sixteen rows.
	$FG,2$Home$FG$ $FG,2$End$FG$	 First and last row.

$FG,5$Editing$FG$
	$FG,2$Ins$FG$		Insert a row in this channel, pushing the rest down.
	$FG,2$Shift-Del$FG$	Delete a row in this channel, pulling the rest up.
	$FG,2$F12$FG$		Edit mode on and off. In view mode the note keys
			audition without writing anything.

$FG,5$Transport$FG$
	$FG,2$Space$FG$	Play or stop the song.
	$FG,2$Enter$FG$	Play the current pattern from the cursor row.
	$FG,2$F5$FG$	Play the song from the start.
	$FG,2$F6$FG$	Play the current pattern, looping.
	$FG,2$F8$FG$	Stop.
	$FG,2$F7$FG$	Follow. The cursor tracks playback.

$FG,5$Panes and files$FG$
	Each pane is a window of its own, listed on the taskbar and stacked with
	everything else. Any number can be open at once, and the key that opened
	one closes it again. They read and write the same song the grid does, so
	an edit in a pane shows in the grid immediately.

	Panes wear a frame and nothing else: no $FG,2$MENU$FG$, no title, no
	$FG,2$[X]$FG$ over the border line. The frame is the kernel's own, thin
	while the window is unfocused and solid while it is focused, in the accent
	colour. Drag the top border to move a pane. Close one with the key or the
	menu entry that opened it, or by clicking the right-hand end of its top
	border, where a window's $FG,2$[X]$FG$ sits.

$FG,8$Turning on Appearance, Flat menu bar in Settings puts the title row back over the top border of every window, panes included; that row is drawn by the window manager rather than by the app.$FG$

	Nothing is left over inside the frame because every pane is a whole number
	of text cells, which is what a window is sized in.

	The keyboard belongs to whichever window is focused: click a pane and the
	cursor keys move inside it, click the grid and they move the cursor.

	$FG,2$F1$FG$	Key list.
	$FG,2$F2$FG$	Instruments.
	$FG,2$F3$FG$	Order list.
	$FG,2$F4$FG$	Macro editor.
	$FG,2$Shift-F4$FG$ Wavetable editor.
	$FG,2$F9$FG$	Save.
	$FG,2$F10$FG$	Load a song or a module.
	$FG,2$F11$FG$	Load a WAV into the current instrument slot.

$FG,5$Song settings$FG$
	$FG,2$- =$FG$	Previous and next pattern.
	$FG,2$[ ]$FG$	Tempo down and up.
	$FG,2$; '$FG$	Speed down and up, in ticks per row.
	$FG,2$, .$FG$	Master gain down and up.
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"6. Instruments"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
Open the instrument pane with $FG,2$F2$FG$. Slots 1 to 6 start out holding built-in waveforms: sine, two pulse widths, triangle, noise and saw. $FG,2$F11$FG$ loads an 8 or 16 bit PCM WAV into the selected slot.

The built-in waves are generated into ordinary looping samples, so everything that works on a recorded sample works on them too: portamento, vibrato, arpeggio, panning.

$FG,5$Macros$FG$
	A macro is a per-tick sequence with a loop point and a release
	point, the idea FamiTracker and DefleMask are built around. Four
	of them per instrument: volume, arpeggio, pitch and duty.

	While a key is held the sequence loops within the section before
	the release point. On note off it jumps past the release point,
	runs to the end and holds. That is what gives chiptune
	instruments their shape without a single effect column entry.

$FG,5$Duty and wavetables$FG$
	The duty macro selects among the four NES pulse widths, 12.5,
	25, 50 and 75 per cent, or among sixteen user wavetables of 32
	four-bit steps in the Game Boy and PC Engine style.

	Every wave in a bank is the same length, so switching mid-note
	keeps the phase and does not click.

$FG,5$Editing macros$FG$
	$FG,2$F4$FG$ opens the macro editor for the selected instrument. The
	sequence is drawn as bars, one column per step.

	$FG,2$Left$FG$ $FG,2$Right$FG$	 Move the step cursor.
	$FG,2$Up$FG$ $FG,2$Down$FG$	 Change the value. Hold $FG,2$Shift$FG$ for steps of 8.
	$FG,2$Tab$FG$		 Next macro: volume, arpeggio, pitch, duty.
	$FG,2$Ins$FG$ $FG,2$Del$FG$	 Lengthen and shorten the sequence.
	$FG,2$L$FG$		 Set or clear the loop point at the cursor.
	$FG,2$R$FG$		 Set or clear the release point.
	$FG,2$M$FG$		 Arpeggio mode: absolute, fixed, relative.
	$FG,2$Click$FG$ or $FG,2$drag$FG$ in the graph to draw freehand.

	The loop marker is green, the release marker orange. Raising a
	value past the end of the sequence extends it.

	Volume runs 0 to 64. Arpeggio and pitch are signed. Duty picks an
	entry from the instrument's wave bank.

$FG,5$Editing wavetables$FG$
	$FG,2$Shift-F4$FG$ opens the wavetable editor. Same movement keys.
	$FG,2$Tab$FG$ steps through the sixteen tables, and a hex digit $FG,2$0-F$FG$ types
	a value straight in. Edits re-render immediately, so a table being
	drawn while the song plays is heard as it changes.

	To use one: set the instrument's bank to Wave and point its duty
	macro at the table number.

$FG,8$While either editor is open it takes the keyboard, so the piano keys do not enter notes into the pattern behind it. Press F4 or Shift-F4 again to close.$FG$
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"5b. Samples"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
	$FG,2$F2$FG$ opens the instrument list. The strip along the bottom draws
	the selected sample's waveform: minimum and maximum per column, so a
	long sample shows its envelope rather than whichever frames a stride
	happened to land on. The two marks in the secondary colour are the
	loop start and end, which is most of what you want to see when picking
	a sample.
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"6a. Arrangement"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
	$FG,2$[ARR ]$FG$ in the top bar, $FG,2$Ctrl-Tab$FG$, or View/Arrangement.

	Channels become lanes down the screen and the order list becomes time
	across it. Each box is a $FG,2$clip$FG$: the pattern that lane plays at
	that position. The bar inside it is how many notes the clip holds, so a
	busy lane looks busy.

	$FG,2$Click a clip$FG$	select it, then drag to another position or lane
	$FG,2$Click a lane name$FG$ mute or unmute that channel
	$FG,2$Arrows$FG$		move the selection
	$FG,2$PgUp$FG$ $FG,2$PgDn$FG$	change which pattern the clip plays
	$FG,2$Del$FG$		back to the shared order list
	$FG,2$Home$FG$ $FG,2$End$FG$	zoom in and out; hold Shift for lane height

	Each clip draws its own notes, pitch against time, scaled to the range
	that clip actually uses, so a bass part fills its box instead of hugging
	the floor. Squeeze the lanes below about twelve pixels and it falls back
	to a density bar, which still shows where the music is.

$FG,5$Wheel$FG$
	$FG,2$Wheel$FG$		scroll: lanes here, pitch in the roll, rows in the grid
	$FG,2$Shift-Wheel$FG$	the other axis: time here and in the roll,
			channels in the grid
	$FG,2$Ctrl-Wheel$FG$	zoom horizontally
	$FG,2$Ctrl-Shift-Wheel$FG$ zoom vertically

	Normally every channel plays the one pattern the order list names, which
	is what MOD, XM, S3M and IT store. Giving a lane its own pattern is an
	$FG,2$override$FG$, drawn in the secondary colour. Setting a clip back to
	the shared pattern removes the override rather than storing a duplicate,
	so a song only carries an arrangement where it actually differs.

	Clips are aliases. Two positions holding the same pattern number are one
	pattern, and an edit to either shows in both. $FG,2$Ctrl-drag$FG$ copies
	first, so the dragged clip becomes its own pattern and the source keeps
	playing what it did. $FG,2$Ctrl-click$FG$ without dragging does the same
	in place, which is the way to make one position of a repeated pattern
	editable on its own.

$FG,5$Mixer strip$FG$
	Each lane header carries a volume fader with the channel's level behind
	it, and a pan slider reading $FG,2$C$FG$ at centre and $FG,2$L$FG$ or
	$FG,2$R$FG$ with a throw out of 64 either side. Click or drag either;
	clicking the header anywhere else mutes. Zoom the lanes shorter than
	about twenty-two pixels and the strip gives way to the channel name.

	$FG,2$Right-click$FG$ a channel to solo it, and again to release every
	mute. The arrangement lane headers, the monitor row above the grid and
	the piano roll all take it.

	Pan snaps to centre. The slider is about five pan units per pixel, so
	dead centre cannot be hit by hand, and centre is the value that matters.

	Both sit AFTER everything the module does. $FG,2$Cxx$FG$ and
	$FG,2$8xx$FG$ own the channel's own volume and pan and overwrite them
	mid-note, so a fader writing there would be undone by the next effect
	column that touches the channel. The fader scales the channel's output
	and the slider shifts its position, and neither is saved with the song.

$FG,8$Overrides are saved in YTK3 version 4. Older songs load with every lane following the order list, which is exactly what they meant. The other formats have no per-channel arrangement, so an imported module starts with none.$FG$
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"6b. Piano roll"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
	Clicking a note sounds it, and dragging one across pitches sounds each
	as it lands, so the roll can be played by ear rather than read. The
	preview uses the note's own instrument, or the editor's when the cell
	names none.

	While the song plays, the note under the playhead stays lit in the
	secondary accent for as long as it sounds, not just on the row that
	started it.

	$FG,2$Ctrl-Tab$FG$ swaps the pattern grid for a piano roll of the
	current channel, and back. It is the same song either way: the
	roll draws the cells the grid edits, so an edit in one shows in
	the other immediately.

	Time runs left to right in rows, pitch bottom to top. A cell
	stores a pitch and never a duration, so a note's block runs
	until the next note, note-off or cut on that channel.

	$FG,2$Ctrl-Tab$FG$	cycles grid, arrangement, roll
	$FG,2$View$FG$ menu	jump straight to any of the three
	$FG,2$Esc$FG$		back to the grid
	$FG,2$View/PianoRoll$FG$	the same toggle from the menu

	$FG,2$Click empty space$FG$	place the current instrument there, then
				drag right to set how long it is
	$FG,2$Drag a note$FG$	move it in time and pitch, keeping its length
	$FG,2$Drag its last column$FG$ change its length
	$FG,2$Click a note's own row$FG$ remove it
	$FG,2$Ctrl-PgUp/PgDn$FG$	scroll the pitch range

Length is not stored in a cell. A note sounds until the next note, note off or cut on that channel, so dragging a note longer writes the $FG,2$^^-$FG$ that ends it, and dragging it shorter moves that off earlier. An off already in the song is left alone.

The other channels show as dim bars so you can see what you are writing against. They are not clickable; the current channel owns the pointer.

$FG,8$Esc leaves the roll rather than quitting the app. Shift-Esc still quits from either view.$FG$
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"7. Formats"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
	$FG,2$.MOD$FG$	ProTracker and friends. 4 to 32 channels, both the
		31 and 15 sample layouts.
	$FG,2$.XM$FG$	FastTracker 2, version 0104. Multi-sample
		instruments, volume and panning envelopes, both the
		linear and Amiga frequency tables.
	$FG,2$.S3M$FG$	Scream Tracker 3. C2SPD tuning, the full letter
		effect set.
	$FG,2$.IT$FG$	Impulse Tracker, sample and instrument modes, new and
		old instrument layouts, sustain loops, new note actions,
		and IT214/215 compressed samples.
	$FG,2$.FTM$FG$	FamiTracker, including the 0CC and Dn forks. The NES
		2A03 only: two pulses, triangle, noise and DPCM. Instrument
		sequences arrive as macros, so volume, arpeggio, pitch and
		duty all play. Noise is not pitched: as on the 2A03 the note
		picks one of sixteen periods, and $FG,2$Vxx$FG$ switches the
		LFSR between its long and short forms. On a pulse channel
		$FG,2$Vxx$FG$ sets the width. Expansion chips (VRC6, VRC7,
		FDS, MMC5, N163, S5B) are parsed for alignment but not
		sounded, and only the first track of a multi-track file
		loads.
	$FG,2$YTK3$FG$	YTracker's own format. Keeps everything, including
		macros and wavetables.
	$FG,2$YTK2$FG$	Songs from the first version of YTracker, imported.

	Not supported: Adlib and OPL instruments, IT's resonant filter
	and Zxx MIDI macros, XM versions below 0104, FamiTracker
	grooves and its 0.5.0-beta TRACK layout, and FamiTracker's
	hardware sweep (Hxy, Ixy), length counter (Exx), DPCM delta
	counter (Zxx) and DPCM retrigger (Xxx).

$FG,8$Detection is by content. A module named .txt still loads; a text file named .mod still does not.$FG$
$ID,-2$

$HL,1$$TR,"8. If something sounds wrong"$$HL,0$
$ID,2$
	$FG,2$Silence on load$FG$	Check the instrument pane. A module using
			a feature this build skips loads with those slots
			empty rather than refusing the whole file.

	$FG,2$Starts quiet$FG$	Some songs open with a global volume fade-in
			that takes half a minute. Watch the third line;
			if the song is fading up, wait.

	$FG,2$Distorted$FG$	Turn the master gain down with $FG,2$,$FG$. Gain is set
			automatically from the channel count, but a
			loud module can still clip.

	$FG,2$Wrong octave$FG$	Notes are numbered so C-4 is middle C and
			matches OpenMPT. ProTracker's own C-1 shows here
			as C-3, which is correct, not a bug.
$ID,-2$

$FG,5$See also$FG$
$ID,2$
$LK,"Char Overview",A="FI:::/Doc/CharOverview.DD"$
